<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pannonhalma Archabbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/996">996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pannonhalma Archabbey is a Benedictine monastery on a hill in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, founded in 996 under Grand Prince Géza and richly endowed by Stephen I. One of the oldest institutions in Hungary, it preserves the founding charter of the abbey of Tihany of 1055, which contains the earliest surviving Hungarian words. It remains an active monastery and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pannonhalma-archabbey.jpg" alt="Pannonhalma Archabbey" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pannonhalma_-_Benc%C3%A9s_ap%C3%A1ts%C3%A1g.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pannonhalma Archabbey, formally the Territorial Abbey of Saint Martin on Mount Pannonhalma, stands on a hill 282 metres high near the town of Pannonhalma and is one of the oldest historical monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Saint Martin of Tours is believed to have been born at the foot of the hill, which was once called the Mount of Saint Martin. It is the second largest territorial abbey in the world, after the one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>. Its sights include the basilica with its crypt, the cloisters, a monumental library with 400,000 volumes and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> refectory with several trompe-l'œil paintings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Prince Géza founded it in 996 as the first Hungarian Benedictine monastery, in honour of Saint Martin of Tours, and it soon became the centre of the Benedictine order. Géza's son, King Stephen I, donated estates and privileges, and Astrik served as its first abbot. The Charter of the Tihany Benedictine Abbey, of 1055, the oldest surviving document to use the Hungarian language, is still preserved in the library. In 1096 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a>, leading one of three crusader armies, spent a week there negotiating safe passage through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> with King Coloman. The first buildings were destroyed in 1137 and rebuilt, the present church dates from the early 13th century, and in 1486 the abbey was reconstructed in Gothic style under King Matthias.</p><p>It became an archabbey in 1541 and was fortified because of Ottoman incursions. During the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> the monks had to abandon the abbey for varying periods. A major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> construction was under way in the time of Archabbot Benedek Sajghó (1722–1768), and the abbey received its present form in 1832, with the library and a classicist tower. It was closed in 1786 and reopened in 1802. In 1950 the communist state confiscated the properties of the order and its schools, which were not returned until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> ended.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The abbey and its surroundings were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1996, a year after the complex was entirely reconstructed and renovated for the millennium. Its archives hold one of the richest collections of documents from the first centuries of Hungarian statehood, among them the interpolated charter of Saint Stephen of 1001–1002 and the founding charter of Tihany. A Benedictine high school, a College of Theology named after Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-csanad">Gerard of Csanád</a> and a winery are among its present uses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pannonhalma Archabbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He also supported Benedictine abbeys such as <b>Pannonhalma</b>, begun under Géza in 996.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
